we haven't talked about autonomous  vehicles for a while we've talked a lot  about the coming uh AI revolution but we  have news out of Michigan specifically  in terms of autonomous vehicles Michigan  has approved a series of bills which  will allow driverless cars with no  passengers and with I'm sorry but with  yes with no steering wheels and without  passengers to be on public roadways and  this is Governor Rick Snyder who has  signed for bills a spark part of what is  being called an autonomous vehicle  legislative package whereas before you  could only do testing of the vehicles by  manufacturers he now is joining the  primary bill a sponsor senator mike cole  as well as executives from General  Motors and Ford and as I told you  earlier in the year pittsburgh has  started live tests of driverless uber  taxis and this is a joint sort of  venture with ford fusions that are that  are uber vehicles they still require  having obviously a steering wheel and  someone sitting a live driver sitting in  the Pat in the driver's seat ready to  take over if anything happens but we're  seeing legislation expand to many states  now 16 states introduce legislation  related to autonomous cars in 2015 12  states did in 2014 and since 2012 we've  seen some kind of autonomous vehicle  registration in 34 states and DC we also  saw a few months ago the National  Highway Transportation and Safety  Administration create some guidelines  for carmakers which talk about sort of  frameworks for what should be in  autonomous vehicle legislation there's  one bill that for example exempts  mechanics from damage to vehicles that  results from repairs assuming that they  were made with manufacturer guidelines  and this is starting to sort of fill out  the lack of coherent law around  liability and on  this vehicles who is responsible if an  autonomous vehicle does X Y or Z is it  the company that designed the logic that  is followed by the autonomous vehicle is  it the individuals at the company that  designed the logic they're very it very  unlikely that it would go that way and  this applies to mechanics and repairs  and so many other areas I still find it  very funny that we still have people in  our audience who will contact me anytime  we talk about autonomous cars this has  been happening for years we've been  talking about autonomous cars off and on  for years I get angry email from people  saying David it's not happening it's  never going to happen we're not going to  have autonomous cars at one point it was  sort of being unwilling to see the  trends now it's just straight up  ignoring of reality there are autonomous  vehicles already operating we have a  driverless truck corridor in Nevada in  the United States that is being tested  with autonomous large transportation  vehicles big rigs as they're often known  it's happening period and at this point  to deny the reality it's sort of like  denying the reality that automation is  going to kill jobs and pretending like  everything's going to remain the same we  would all be so much better off if we  accepted the reality and started  thinking about the big questions that  these new realities will raise uh the  biggest hurdle what will be the biggest  hurdle for autonomous cars will it be  human acceptance will it be the  technological issues that I generally  believe are going to be worked out is it  going to be uh lobbying groups that are  very much against it probably some  combination of all of these things
 

